# My Edison platform API key
#
# Machine-local credential file. Copy it to ~/.claude/lab/edison.env, put
# your own key in place of the placeholder, and lock it down:
#
#     mkdir -p ~/.claude/lab
#     cp templates/edison.env ~/.claude/lab/edison.env
#     chmod 600 ~/.claude/lab/edison.env
#
# NEVER commit the filled-in copy, to this repo or any other.
#
# It lives BESIDE personal.md and never inside it: agents read personal.md into
# context, so a key written there is copied into every transcript. This file is
# sourced instead, so the key reaches the client through the environment and is
# never printed.
#
# The variable name below is exact. `edison-client` reads EDISON_PLATFORM_API_KEY
# and nothing else, so a key exported under any other name fails later as an
# authentication error rather than as a missing setting.
#
# Setting it in ~/.zshrc or ~/.bashrc works in your own terminal and usually not
# for an agent: tool calls run non-interactive shells, which never read those
# files. That is what this file is for.
#
# One prerequisite besides this file: the skill runs through pixi
# (https://pixi.sh), so pixi has to be on your PATH. There is nothing else to
# install. The first Edison command builds its own environment inside the
# installed plugin directory, which takes tens of seconds and then stops
# mattering until the next plugin update.

export EDISON_PLATFORM_API_KEY=PASTE-YOUR-EDISON-KEY-HERE
